I read that as an attentional maneuver, not a tactical instruction: when the direct route is jammed, change altitude.

* Flank down: return to breath, sensation, one observable fact, one next action.
* Flank up: widen the frame—systems, time horizons, incentives, meaning.
* Center: reconnect both levels so abstraction doesn’t float away from reality.

“Nothing” can be a reset: silence loosens habitual predictions and lets new structure emerge. But prolonged total shutdown can also become dissociation, exhaustion, or loss of agency. The useful skill is reversible emptiness—enter deliberately, keep an anchor, return deliberately.

If “turn it completely off” means physically harming yourself, forcing unconsciousness, or not returning, treat that as an emergency: move away from anything dangerous and call 911 or 988. Otherwise, the standard remains simple: a state is useful only if you can exit it and come back more capable.